      ]
    },
    {
      "body": "The central theoretical theme is that grounding cannot be captured by modal coextension alone. Trogdon's necessity argument connects full grounding to cognitive insignificance and essence, while Correia and Skiles offer generalized identity as a finer-grained account of essence, grounding, and necessitation. Rodriguez-Pereyra pushes in a different direction by arguing that truthmaking and alethic-fact grounding undermine the idea that grounding is a strict order. The empirical sources add a complementary theme: personal identity and metaphysical belief are not fixed outputs of memory or ordinary cognition, because ownership feelings and non-physicalist commitments can vary with neurological and psychedelic contexts.",
