    },
    {
      "body": "The formal sources agree that grounding must be treated as more structured than bare necessitation, but they disagree about which structural constraints survive. One line defends full-grounding necessity by treating apparent counterexamples as partial-ground or non-ground cases. Another accepts grounding as a broad genus but denies that it is transitive, irreflexive, or asymmetric once truthmaking and alethic-fact grounding are included. The generalized-identity account partly mediates the dispute by explaining necessitation through essence while preserving distinctions that modal equivalence misses. The empirical sources do not settle those formal disputes, but they sharpen a parallel lesson: psychological evidence can reveal contingency in identity and belief without by itself deciding the metaphysical order of grounds.",
